# NOBORU CONTENT RULE

Version: 1.0

Priority: CRITICAL

Status: AUTHORITATIVE

This rule governs all educational content usage and assembly within Noboru learning features.

Every lesson, vocabulary item, kanji entry, grammar point, review card, and progression screen must follow this document, docs/content-pipeline.md, docs/jlpt-content-architecture.md, and docs/NOBORU LEARNING ARCHITECTURE BIBLE.md.

Educational content is sacred.

It must never live in JSX as the source of truth.

⸻

PURPOSE

The purpose of this rule is to ensure:

* Educational content originates from authoritative systems
* UI renders content, it does not own it
* JLPT progression remains coherent and documented
* Features assemble content without duplicating domain ownership
* Content updates do not require code deploys for routine editorial changes
* Learning quality remains consistent as content volume grows

Content systems feed the climb.

UI shows the trail markers.

⸻

REQUIREMENTS

Content Authority

* Database, CMS, and approved content systems are authoritative
* Forbidden: hardcoded vocabulary lists, kanji tables, grammar explanations, or lesson scripts in UI files
* Allowed in UI: loading states, layout, formatting, pedagogy-preserving presentation, and mapping rendered fields

References

* docs/content-pipeline.md defines ingestion, validation, publishing, and update flow
* docs/jlpt-content-architecture.md defines progression, level structure, and educational model
* When documents conflict with architecture.mdc on ownership, architecture.mdc wins

Domain Ownership

* vocabulary owns vocabulary data
* kanji owns kanji data
* grammar owns grammar data
* learning assembles lessons and paths
* review assembles sessions from owned domain data
* Lessons do not own underlying content entities

Progression

* JLPT levels and mastery progression follow docs/jlpt-content-architecture.md
* Runtime pedagogy (pools, golden rule, session mix, lifecycle) follows docs/NOBORU LEARNING ARCHITECTURE BIBLE.md via lib/learning/
* Educational progress is authoritative
* Gamification may read progress but must not define it

Layering

Component

↓

Feature service

↓

Repository

↓

Content tables or CMS-backed source

UI must not bypass services to query content tables directly.

Offline

* Core learning content must be available offline after sync or download as defined by architecture
* Offline caches store content snapshots, not alternate authoritative copies without merge rules

Validation

* Content consumed by services must be validated against feature schemas
* Reject malformed content at service boundary with logged errors

Testing

* Use fixtures representing content shape, not production content copied into tests as hidden source of truth

⸻

CODING STANDARDS

Services

* learning.service.ts assembles lesson views from owned domain services
* vocabulary.service.ts, kanji.service.ts, grammar.service.ts own retrieval and rules for their domains
* review.service.ts builds queues from progress and content records

Components

* Render props or view models only
* Format readings, furigana, examples, and hints via dedicated presentation components
* No embedded lesson copy except static UI chrome unrelated to curriculum

Types

* Content types live in feature types/ directories
* Separate transport rows from presentation view models where useful

Updates

* Content pipeline changes must update docs/content-pipeline.md when process changes
* Schema or progression changes must update docs/jlpt-content-architecture.md

⸻

SUCCESS CRITERIA

Content work is successful when:

* Curriculum changes can flow through content systems without UI edits
* Each domain owns its entities clearly
* Lessons and reviews assemble from services, not static arrays
* JLPT progression matches documented architecture
* Offline mode uses synced authoritative content
* UI remains pedagogy-focused and formatting-aware
* Documentation reflects actual content flow

⸻

FAILURE CRITERIA

Content quality is degraded when any of the following occur:

* Vocabulary or kanji hardcoded in components
* Lesson order encoded only in page files
* Review queues built from inline mock arrays in production paths
* Multiple features storing conflicting copies of the same word or kanji
* Learning progress derived from gamification tables
* Content validation skipped at service boundaries
* Editorial updates requiring code changes for routine text changes
* Undocumented JLPT progression logic in hooks

When failure criteria appear, move content back to authoritative systems before adding new lessons.

⸻

EXAMPLES

Correct rendering

VocabularyCard receives VocabularyViewModel from vocabularyService.getItem(id).

Correct lesson assembly

learningService.getLesson(lessonId) composes vocabulary, grammar, and kanji references by ID.

Correct test fixture

Minimal fake VocabularyItem object in tests/content.fixture.ts.

Incorrect production code

const words = [{ jp: "食べる", en: "to eat" }, ...] inside a lesson component.

Incorrect ownership

learning feature defining canonical kanji stroke metadata inline.

Incorrect review source

Review queue built from hardcoded array in page.tsx.

⸻

ANTI-PATTERNS

Hardcoded educational content in UI

Copy-pasted JLPT word lists across features

Lesson scripts in React components

Direct content table queries from components

Multiple sources of truth for the same grammar point

Gamification defining lesson unlock authority

CMS-bypassing "temporary" inline content that ships to production

Unvalidated JSON content rendered directly into lesson UI

Feature-local content edits without pipeline documentation

Using test fixtures as shadow production curriculum

